window.SEARCH_DOCS = [{"id":0,"url":"index.html","title":"Authentic Memory Docs","section":"","text":"Authentic Memory docs This manual describes how our technology is designed and built. Authentic Memory is protecting historical records in a synthetic age. We provide non-profit infrastructure to ensure that humanity’s historical record remains distinguishable from AI-generated content. If you are new to Authentic Memory , check out a demonstration and explanation of how it works. If you would like to verify an Authentic Memory proof record visit verify.authenticmemory.org . If you manage an archival collection and would like to use Authentic Memory anchoring , see this explanation and instructions . If you manage an archival collection and would first like to understand how Authentic Memory works before committing to a collaboration, this technology overview page provides the clearest explanation. Otherwise, this manual provides the most detailed and current technical documentation ."},{"id":1,"url":"requirements/index.html","title":"Software requirements","section":"","text":"Software requirements"},{"id":2,"url":"requirements/standards/index.html","title":"Standards","section":"Software requirements","text":"Standards"},{"id":3,"url":"requirements/theory/index.html","title":"Theory and research","section":"Software requirements","text":"Theory and research"},{"id":4,"url":"requirements/user/index.html","title":"User requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"User requirements"},{"id":5,"url":"requirements/data/index.html","title":"Data requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Data requirements"},{"id":6,"url":"requirements/functional/index.html","title":"Functional requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Functional requirements"},{"id":7,"url":"requirements/quality/index.html","title":"Quality requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Quality requirements"},{"id":8,"url":"requirements/quality/security.html","title":"Security","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Security"},{"id":9,"url":"requirements/quality/performance.html","title":"Performance","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Performance"},{"id":10,"url":"requirements/quality/scalability.html","title":"Scalability","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Scalability"},{"id":11,"url":"decisions/index.html","title":"Architectural decisions","section":"","text":"Architectural decisions This section explains the architecture options and decisions made by the Authentic Memory team. They are each presented as an Architectural Decision Record (ADR). In this section Decision Records Component model Data model"},{"id":12,"url":"decisions/anchoring/index.html","title":"Anchoring","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"This is a register that records of all the technical architecture decisions made by Authentic Memory to implement its solutions."},{"id":13,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-010-bitcoin-blockchain-time-anchor.html","title":"ADR 010: Bitcoin blockchain time anchor","section":"Architectural decisions › Anchoring","text":"ADR 010: Bitcoin blockchain time anchor Description Use the Bitcoin blockchain as the primary time anchor for Authentic Memory proof records. Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference The core guarantee of Authentic Memory’s Open Proof Record is existence : a publicly verifiable lower bound on the date by which a file existed in exactly its current form. The evidentiary value of that lower bound depends on when it is created, because a timestamp made before indistinguishable synthetic media became possible supports an inference that a later one cannot. The time anchor therefore has to: remain verifiable for decades, beyond the lifetime of Authentic Memory and of any single institution; be checkable by anyone, without an account, fee, permission or trust in Authentic Memory; resist retroactive alteration, including by well-resourced adversaries such as organised denial campaigns. Decision Anchor every Open Proof Record in the Bitcoin blockchain. The hash of each signed OPR (or of a Merkle root over a batch of them) is committed to a Bitcoin transaction through the OpenTimestamps protocol ( ADR-020 ). The resulting proof is stored in the OPR’s timestamps array. Rationale Cost of rewriting history grows with time. Under Bitcoin’s proof-of-work model, the cost of rewriting confirmed transactions grows exponentially with their age. Track record. Bitcoin has operated continuously for over fifteen years without a successful attack on confirmed blocks. Permissionless and public. It is fully public and independently verifiable without any account, fee or permission. No maintaining authority. Long-term temporal proof rests on historical block headers, which are immutable, verifiable offline and bound to no authority that has to survive. A record stays verifiable even if Authentic Memory as an organisation does not. Retroactive protection for signatures. Because the anchor fixes the date, a future break of Ed25519 cannot produce a forged signature that appears to have been anchored in a 2026 block. Assumptions Bitcoin block headers will remain publicly available and the chain’s proof-of-work security will continue to hold for the evidentiary lifetime of the proofs. SHA-256, on which both Bitcoin and OpenTimestamps depend, remains collision- and pre-image-resistant (see ADR-030 ). Partner institutions, funders and public authorities will accept a public blockchain as a time anchor. Alternatives Considered RFC 3161 timestamping authorities and certificate chains (the C2PA model). Rejected as the primary anchor: their validity depends on authorities and certificates staying valid, requiring periodic re-timestamping and preservation of validation data, an obligation that cannot lapse over fifty to a hundred years ( relationship to C2PA ). RFC 3161 remains a candidate additional anchor (risks T-08, T-11). bloXberg (scientific blockchain). Considered as a supplementary ledger for redundancy, not as a replacement ( system workflow ). Certificate Transparency logs, a GAMI transparency log and AT Protocol. Proposed in the to-be architecture as additional, independent anchors, not as substitutes ( system overview ). Zeitwerk (federated hourly co-signatures). Would suit a second anchor if it became established infrastructure, but GAMI will not be directly involved as a prominent co-signer or ask its institutions to sign, and the project will probably be discontinued ( competing options ). Consequences / Implications Easier: proofs can be validated with only the GPR, the file, the institution’s public key and public Bitcoin headers; GAMI is not in the validity path. Latency: anchoring is not instantaneous. Proofs are “pending” until a Bitcoin block confirms them and must then be upgraded (see ADR-002 ). Lower bound only: the anchor proves existence no later than the block date. It says nothing about integrity before anchoring (Tier 2 evidence) or about content truthfulness. Single anchor today: every live proof rests on one time source. The funder commitment of a second independent timestamping system by 31 Dec 2026 is open (D-13, risk T-08, requirement U-36). Offline verification: the CLI verifier reports Bitcoin membership as indeterminate without chain data (risk T-03). Adoption risk: some public authorities may reject Bitcoin or require a sovereign alternative (risk B-10). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-002 : OpenTimestamps as the anchoring protocol. ADR-003 : SHA-256 file fingerprint. ADR-006 : timestamps array allows more than one anchor per GPR. Open decision D-13: choice of the second timestamping system. Risks T-03, T-08, T-10, B-10, B-16 in the risk register ."},{"id":14,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-015-rfc-3161-time-anchor.html","title":"ADR 015: RFC 3161 second time anchor","section":"Architectural decisions › Anchoring","text":"ADR 015: RFC 3161 second time anchor Title Use the RFC 3161 timestamps as a secondary time anchor for Authentic Memory proof records. Status Proposed Context / Requirement Reference Decision Rationale Assumptions Alternatives Considered"},{"id":15,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-020-opentimestamps-anchor.html","title":"ADR 020: Opentimestamps anchoring.md","section":"Architectural decisions › Anchoring","text":"ADR 020: Opentimestamps anchoring.md Title Anchor OPRs to Bitcoin through OpenTimestamps calendars with Merkle aggregation Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference ADR-010 selects Bitcoin as the time anchor. Writing one Bitcoin transaction per record is impractical: institutions may anchor tens of millions of files per year, and each transaction carries a fee and confirmation delay. A mechanism is needed that commits very large numbers of digests to Bitcoin cheaply, produces a portable proof per record, and can be verified with open tooling. Decision The institutional portal aggregates the digests of signed OPRs in a signing batch into a Merkle tree and submits them to the public OpenTimestamps (OTS) calendars . The calendars aggregate submissions from all users into a further Merkle tree and commit its root in a Bitcoin transaction (C8). Submission goes to multiple calendar servers with automatic retry . The portal later upgrades pending proofs to complete Bitcoin proofs once the commitment is confirmed. The OTS proof is stored in the OPR’s timestamps array with its method, status, submission date and, once confirmed, block height. Rationale OpenTimestamps is an open protocol with independent implementations, usable without an account or fee. Aggregation lets a single Bitcoin commitment cover arbitrarily many records, so anchoring cost does not grow with collection size. OTS uses SHA-256, the same hash used for file fingerprints ( ADR-030 ). A completed OTS proof is verified against Bitcoin block headers alone. The calendars are needed to create and upgrade proofs, not to verify them. Assumptions The public, volunteer-run OTS calendars continue to accept submissions and publish their commitments to Bitcoin. At least one calendar is reachable at submission time. The automatic retry absorbs temporary outages. The portal’s upgrade job runs until every pending proof is confirmed. Alternatives Considered Authentic Memory writing its own Bitcoin transactions, or running its own calendar. Not recorded as evaluated for the current release. An institution-run OTS calendar or Bitcoin node appears in the to-be architecture as a planned sovereign anchoring path. Non-Bitcoin anchors are covered in ADR-010 . Consequences / Implications Easier: anchoring scales to large batches at negligible marginal cost. Harder: the OTS calendars are a dependency for creating proofs, not for verifying them. Until a pending proof is upgraded (usually within hours), it relies on at least one of the calendars that received it. If all fail, the batch is resubmitted with a later date (risk T-10). Overdue pending proofs need alerting. Running Authentic Memory’s own calendar, estimated at €10k a year including the operator, would remove the creation dependency. The Bitcoin chain stores only a Merkle root, so a lost OPR cannot be rebuilt from the blockchain. Institution-held copies and replication are essential (risk T-04). The service depends on volunteer infrastructure it does not control (risk B-16). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-010 : Bitcoin as time anchor."},{"id":16,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-030-sha256-file-fingerprint.html","title":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory","section":"Architectural decisions › Anchoring","text":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory ADR-003-sha256-file-fingerprint.md Title Use SHA-256, computed locally or reused from existing fixity records, as the file fingerprint Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference A GAMI proof must identify one exact file without the file ever leaving institutional custody ( what we deliberately do not do ). Collections reach the petabyte range, so the fingerprint must be fast to compute and, where possible, reuse work institutions have already done. Related requirements: U-02 (calculate digests locally without changing source files), U-03 (reuse trustworthy existing digests), U-04 (resumable preparation) in system requirements . Decision Fingerprint every file with SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) . The hash is computed inside the institution’s infrastructure, by GAMI Hash ( ADR-011 ) or by the institution’s own systems. Where an OAIS-conformant preservation system already holds SHA-256 fixity values (for example Archivematica, Preservica), those values may be reused instead of rehashing. SHA-256 is also used to hash GPRs for anchoring and in public verification ( ADR-015 ). Rationale From system workflow , system security and system scalability : Suitable properties: determinism, avalanche effect, pre-image resistance and collision resistance. Ecosystem alignment: the same function is used by OpenTimestamps, C2PA, Archivematica and Preservica, so existing fixity hashes can be reused and the GPR digest feeds OTS directly. Long-term security: 128-bit effective security even against quantum search (Grover); no known practical weaknesses. Throughput: about 500 MB/s per CPU core; roughly 1 PB hashed in about three days on one 8-core server. Assumptions SHA-256 remains secure for the evidentiary lifetime of the proofs, or a weakness will be announced early enough for the renewal chain to re-anchor with a stronger algorithm ( post-quantum resilience ). Reused fixity values are current and correctly bound to the file being anchored (challenged by risk T-13). Alternatives Considered No alternative primary hash function is recorded as having been evaluated. SHA-3 is named as the post-quantum re-hashing algorithm for the planned renewal chain, not as a replacement for the file fingerprint ( system security ). Perceptual fingerprinting is described as a future extension for matching re-encoded or cropped derivatives, complementing exact-byte hashing ( matched-fragment attribution ). Consequences / Implications Easier: institutions with SHA-256 fixity skip the hashing phase entirely. Verification needs only the file and standard tooling. Exact-byte matching only: re-encoded, cropped or screenshot copies never match, and readers may read “not found” as “fake” (risk T-07). Fixity reuse is weaker than it looks: only Archivematica stores SHA-256 by default among surveyed systems, and no current pilot uses it (risk T-29). Reused values may be stale or bound to the wrong file (risk T-13), so reconciliation with sample re-hashing before signing is required (U-34). Disclosure: publishing file hashes lets anyone confirm whether a known file is held (risk T-27). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-009 : read-only integration that relies on reusing fixity. ADR-011 : GAMI Hash. ADR-015 : hashing in the reader’s browser. Risks T-07, T-13, T-27, T-29 in the risk register ."},{"id":17,"url":"decisions/cryptography/index.html","title":"Cryptography","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Cryptography"},{"id":18,"url":"decisions/software.md/index.html","title":"Software","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Software"},{"id":19,"url":"architecture/index.html","title":"Architectural design","section":"","text":"Architectural design Integer in mauris eu nibh euismod gravida. Duis ac tellus et risus vulputate vehicula. Donec lobortis risus a elit. Etiam tempor, ut ullamcorper ligula eu tempor congue, eros est euismod turpis. In this section View1 View2"},{"id":20,"url":"architecture/view1.html","title":"View1","section":"Architectural design","text":""},{"id":21,"url":"architecture/view2.html","title":"View2","section":"Architectural design","text":""},{"id":22,"url":"software/index.html","title":"Software components","section":"","text":"Software components"},{"id":23,"url":"software/gami-app/index.html","title":"Gami App","section":"Software components","text":""},{"id":24,"url":"software/gami-hash/index.html","title":"Gami Hash","section":"Software components","text":""},{"id":25,"url":"references/glossary/index.html","title":"Glossary","section":"References","text":"Glossary"},{"id":26,"url":"references/citations/index.html","title":"Citations","section":"References","text":"Citations"}];
